      "id": 1,
      "text": "Dudley was named after an English town and reached its peak growth around 1864 during the Broad Top coal boom.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/dudley_pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Huntingdon and Broad Top Mountain Railroad extended its Shoup's Run branch to Dudley in 1855, making the borough a key coal loading station with a double switchback.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/dudley_pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Pennsylvania Route 913 cuts through Dudley, leading 2.5 miles uphill to Broad Top City and 2 miles downhill to Coalmont.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dudley%2C_Pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Dudley sits in the valley of Shoup Run, a west‑flowing tributary of the Raystown Branch Juniata River.",
